\section*{Possible Questions}
\begin{itemize}
\item Find exact exponential time algorithm running in time $(2 - \epsilon)^n$ for \textsc{Locating Dominating Set} for any $\epsilon > 0$. 
A good starting point would be to check such algorithms for \textsc{Dominating Set}.
\item \textsc{Locating-dominating Set} admits a polynomial time $\calO(\log(n))$-factor 
approximation algorithm.
This translates to $2^{\calO(k)}$-time algorithm that returns a $\calO(k)$-factor approximate solution.
We also know that in $2^{\calO(k^2)}$ time, we can solve the problem, i.e., get an optimum solution.
Hence, the natural question is: Does there exist a algorithm running in time $2^{\calO(k \log (k))}$ 
and returns $(1+\epsilon)$-factor approximation algorithm?
At this point, $c$-factor approximation algorithm for any $c > 1$ is desirable.

This \cite{DBLP:conf/soda/LokshtanovMRSZ21, DBLP:conf/mfcs/EibenHR17} might be good places to start. 
%Especially, \cite{DBLP:conf/mfcs/EibenHR17} contains a trade-off between kernel size and approximation
