+++ b/README.md
@@ -0,0 +1,142 @@
+# Verified Test Cover generator for arXiv:2411.17948
+
+Status: the generator and every local gate pass, but the required four-vendor oracle audit is **not complete**. OpenRouter rejected the bare, structural-hint, and placebo runs with HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded (total limit)` before any attempt counted. The harness-owned error transcripts are retained; they are not evidence that a model failed. Re-run Step 4 with a funded key before treating this family as hardened or emitting it for release.
+
+| Profile field | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | **B — no-tool compression** |
+| Native domain | combinatorics |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | exact cover |
+| Certificate form | integer tuple (a list of triples naming tests) |
+| Intuition | invariant: repeated residue classes expose the core tests |
+| Domain essentiality | licensed reduction |
+| Reduction | paper-licensed, Section 5 Red–Blue Dominating Set → Test Cover |
+
+## What the family is
+
+[Structural Parameterization of Locating-Dominating Set and Test Cover](https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.17948) defines Test Cover by membership signatures. Section 5 replaces each original blue item by a pair, adds binary representation tests with private items plus an isolated item, and proves that the representation tests are forced. A core test distinguishes the two copies of an original item exactly when the corresponding red vertex covers it.
+
+Here the red neighborhoods are target-sum triples of `3n` weighted labels. The generator first creates a strict 3-partition, then carries that held partition through the paper's reduction. The solver receives the resulting finite set system in exact succinct notation and returns the selected core tests as triples. `verify` checks IDs, a full disjoint partition, exact integer sums, and injective nonzero bit signatures; it never reads `inst["answer"]`.
+
+## Why this is Track B
+
+This is deliberately not a Track A claim. Theorem 1 gives general Test Cover a `2^{O(|U| log |U|)}(|U|+|F|)^{O(1)}` algorithm, and Section 5 identifies the reduction used here. On this generated distribution an even simpler quadratic pair-sum index produces every core test. At shipping size `m=168`, it performs 14,028 pair probes and 28,224 counted arithmetic/lookups, solving 8/8 instances in a measured median 0.0177 s. That program is efficient and disclosed.
+
+The no-tool gap is between those 28,224 mechanical operations and the 280-operation compact route: after recognizing the common-modulus residue invariant, equal residues pair with their complementary residue. Across 168 shuffled large weights, executing the mechanical pair scan by hand is not realistic. Knowing the invariant makes the family easy; finding it is the intended task.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=11`)
+
+This is the complete rendered demo statement:
+
+```text
+PAIRED-ITEM TEST COVER
+
+A test is a subset of a finite item set. A selected collection is a test
+cover when every two distinct items have different membership signatures:
+some selected test contains exactly one of them. Tests and items below are
+finite even where a formula names a whole family.
+
+There are m=9 original labels 0 through 8.
+Label i has integer weight w_i, and the target is B=320288.
+Every weight lies strictly between B/4 and B/2. The weights are:
+  0:128769  1:92777  2:95466  3:98742  4:111544  5:99329  6:107209  7:125493
+  8:101535
+
+The Test Cover item set contains an isolated item O; private items
+D_0,...,D_5; and paired items P_i,Q_i for every original label i.
+Each label i has the displayed nonzero binary code c_i=1+i,
+written in exactly 5 bits (least significant bit is bit 1).
+
+The tests are exactly the following:
+* Z_0 contains D_0 and every P_i,Q_i.
+* For t=1,...,5, Z_t contains D_t and contains both P_i,Q_i
+  exactly when bit t of c_i is 1.
+* For every unordered triple T={i,j,k} of distinct original labels whose
+  three weights sum to B, there is one core test C_T. It contains every
+  P_l; it contains Q_l exactly when l is not in T; and it contains no O
+  or D_t. There are no other tests.
+
+Find a test cover using at most K=9 tests.
+The private item D_t and isolated O force every Z_t into any cover. A
+compact certificate therefore lists only the selected core tests, naming
+C_{i,j,k} by [i,j,k]; the decoder adds all Z_t automatically.
+Return exactly n=3 triples. Every original label must occur
+exactly once, and every listed triple must have weight sum exactly B.
+These conditions make the decoded tests a concrete cover of size K.
+IDs are zero-based; repeats are forbidden. Triple order and the order
+inside a triple do not matter.
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags, as a JSON array of
+exactly 3 arrays, each containing exactly three integer IDs.
+Syntax example for a hypothetical six-label instance:
+<answer>[[0,2,4],[1,3,5]]</answer>
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+Answer: `[[0,1,3],[2,5,7],[4,6,8]]`. The demo is hand-solvable: only 280 triple partitions exist, and the three displayed sums can be checked directly.
+
+```python
+verify(inst, [[0,1,3],[2,5,7],[4,6,8]])
+# (True, "ok")
+verify(inst, [[2,1,3],[0,5,7],[4,6,8]])
+# (False, "triple_weight_not_target")
+```
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| Preset | n triples | Original labels | Target multiplier | Modulus | log2 candidate space | Status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 3 | 9 | 32 | 10,009 | 8.13 | hand example; skipped by hardener |
+| easy | 32 | 96 | 40 | 10,009 | 297.90 | oracle not reached: key error |
+| medium | 48 | 144 | 48 | 10,009 | 502.60 | oracle not reached |
+| hard | 56 | 168 | 56 | 2,147,483,647 | 611.15 | provisional shipping preset |
+
+Escalation raises coefficient height at fixed `n=56`, so it grows the arithmetic haystack without lengthening the 168-atom answer.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| Gate | Result | Measurement |
+|---|---|---|
+| G1 | pass | 20/20 planted witnesses; full 128-residue pool checked |
+| G2 | pass | five corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | pass | tagged JSON round-trip; garbage returns `None` |
+| G4 | pass | 0 / 200,000 uniform structure-aware partitions valid |
+| G5 | pass | demo: 1 / 280 exact; reference median 28,224 ops, 0.0177 s |
+| G6 | pass | five attacks at 0/8; reference solver at 8/8 |
+| G7 | pass | doubled `n=112` builds in 0.0162 s and verifies |
+| G8 | pass | 80 invariance + 80 carried-witness checks; 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | pass | 675 chars, 169 estimated tokens, 168 atoms, 280 intended operations |
+
+## Oracle loop and G9 diagnostics
+
+No row below is a scored attempt; every call was an API error and the harness stopped as designed.
+
+| Arm | Preset | Seeds | Scored result | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|---|
+| bare | easy | 1037445779, 790001277, 1082152814, 1452794723 | 0 valid attempts | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| structural | hard | 2070501887, 1149669471, 201843845, 1538463783 | 0 valid attempts | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+| placebo | hard | 1320988186, 1652262022, 2113958108, 582821648 | 0 valid attempts | four HTTP 403 key-limit errors |
+
+Thus bare / hinted / placebo are all `0 solved / 0 attempts`; hinted-minus-placebo is not estimable. The transcripts only establish the external block. They say nothing about whether the residue hint helps.
+
+## Use
+
+```python
+import gen_2411_17948 as g
+
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=11, **g.DIFFICULTY["demo"])
+text = g.render(inst)
+answer = g.parse_answer("<answer>[[0,1,3],[2,5,7],[4,6,8]]</answer>")
+assert g.verify(inst, answer) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After a successful hardening rerun, emit from the repository root with:
+
+```bash
+bash scripts/emit.sh 2411.17948 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+The family is easy with a pair-sum program or once the repeated-residue invariant is recognized; that is exactly why it is Track B. The 0/200,000 estimate is only for the declared uniform prior over unlabeled triple partitions and does not cover a solver using arithmetic correlations. I did not run a SAT/ILP encoding or the paper's full Theorem 1 dynamic program because the quadratic reference already solves every generated instance. The canonical key is a strong invariant for all generated item and bit-test relabelings tested here, not a complete canonical form for arbitrary Test Cover isomorphism. Most importantly, no vendor oracle produced a scored response, so practical no-tool hardness remains unverified until the OpenRouter limit is fixed and all three arms are rerun.
