-pair of items occurs differently in at least one selected test.  Section 3
-represents it by the natural red-test/blue-item auxiliary bipartite graph.
-Theorem 1 and Lemma 4 give a 2^{O(|U| log |U|)} polynomial-space-style
-partition-refinement dynamic program; the Test Cover subsection also records
-the direct |F|^{O(k)} subset enumeration and Bondy's |U|-1 upper bound.  These
-algorithms are why this constructed distribution is Track B, never Track A.
+Construction. A fixed-weight logical mask is sampled, and all of its cyclic
+rotations are formed. A base is rejected before the instance exists unless the
+orbit has rank d. These orbit tests are the answer sampled first. Decoys are
+then sampled from the proper span of the first h orbit rows, conditioned to
+have exactly the same support weight; immediate rotational neighbors are
+excluded at named presets. Finally a random relabelling of coordinate names and
+a random test ordering are applied, carrying the witness. All tests therefore
+have the same cardinality 2^(d-1), the same displayed support size, and (over
+the generator's random coordinate relabelling) the same one-test support
+distribution. The planted/decoy distinction is collective rather than a
+per-test magnitude or size marker.
 
-Paper-licensed transformation.  Section 5, 'Incompressibility of Test Cover',
-reduces a Red-Blue Dominating Set graph G' to Test Cover.  It makes a circ/star
-item pair for each source blue vertex.  A source red test contains both copies
-of every non-neighbour but only the circ copy of each neighbour.  The forced
-bit-representation tests distinguish different pairs, and an isolated item
-forces all of those bit tests into every size-k solution.  Consequently the
-remaining source tests distinguish every circ/star pair exactly when they
-dominate every source blue vertex.
+Easy regimes. The paper states that Test Cover is FPT by solution size, gives
+the |U|-parameter algorithm in Theorem 1, and notes Bondy's |U|-1 upper bound.
+Our succinct parity representation exposes the still easier rank criterion.
+Trees, bounded feedback-edge-set graphs, twin cover, distance to clique, and
+neighbourhood diversity are Locating-Dominating Set regimes from Sections 3--4
+and are not used here.
 
-Construction.  The source blue vertices are the q left and q right vertices
-of a degree-regular bipartite graph; source red vertices are its edges.  Since
-each source test covers exactly two blue vertices, q tests dominate all 2q
-vertices exactly when their edges form a perfect matching.  Before assembling
-the graph, choose b and plant y=2x+b mod q.  The other degree-1 perfect
-matchings are conjugated cyclic shifts, so every left and right source vertex
-has the same degree and every edge is generated as part of a perfect matching.
-The planted vector is then carried through the paper's reduction.  It is not
